Why do Linux permissions exist?

Would you allow everyone to access and modify everything?

Of course not!

Linux uses permissions to control who can:
1. Read a file.
2. Modify a file.
3. Execute a file.
This is called Linux File Permissions.

Linux has three basic permissions.
Symbol	Permission	Meaning
r	Read	View file contents
w	Write	Modify file contents
x	Execute	Run a file as a program or script

Think of a document:
- Read: You can open and view it.
- Write: You can edit it.
- Execute: You can run it if it's an executable program or script.

Linux permissions apply to three categories.
Symbol	Category	Meaning
u	User	File owner
g	Group	Users belonging to the file's group
o	Others	Everyone else

The first character indicates the file type.
-rwxr-xr--
- - = Regular file.
- d = Directory.
- l = Symbolic link.

Understanding chmod
This is one of the most important Linux commands.

chmod means Change Mode.

It changes file and directory permissions.

Understanding numeric permissions
Permission	Number
Read (r)	4
Write (w)	2
Execute (x)	1

Understanding chown
chown means Change Owner.
Change ownership recursively: chown -R
Be careful with recursive ownership changes. They affect everything inside the target directory.

